 \begin{abstract}
-We study the construction of an equivalent acyclic combinational gate network from a complete multi-output truth table under input-dependent costs for two-input AND, two-input OR and NOT gates. Our design combines Shannon decomposition and shared reduced ordered binary decision diagrams with representation diversification, complemented-edge sharing, affine recognition, cost-aware rewriting, and timing-constrained area recovery. The engineering implementation subsequently eliminates repeated BDD and implicant computations and replaces text-oriented intermediate transformations with a typed in-memory DAG. On the 25 released Stage 1 instances, the frozen Round 5.1 candidate passes every official functional check and meets every delay target, with aggregate gate area 529,014. In two paired WSL evaluations against its Round 5 predecessor, the reported median synthesis runtime changes from 72.188 to 55.488 ms and from 60.827 to 55.077 ms, without changing reported case-level area or delay. We also evaluate bounded four-input cut rewriting: it reduces area in new generated families, but fails a predeclared runtime-cost criterion on the local Stage 1 evaluation. We separate Boolean-equivalence proofs from empirical performance observations, document the validation protocol, and make no claim of global optimality or hidden-case generalization.
+A complete truth table determines a Boolean function but not a low-cost, timing-feasible implementation. We examine how a course-project synthesizer can progress from an exact Shannon/ROBDD construction to a shared, priced gate DAG under AND2/OR2/NOT costs and a synthesis-time budget. The first problem is architectural: varying decision orders and mapping forms eliminates the baseline's timing-target misses. The second is global: complemented functions, shared fanout and required-time analysis let the mapper trade local delay for area without discarding correctness. The third is computational: caching and typed in-memory graphs reduce redundant synthesis work. On all 25 released Stage 1 cases, our frozen Round 5.1 research candidate passes the official checks, meets every delay target, and has aggregate area 529,014. Two paired WSL runs against Round 5 give median runtimes of 72.188 versus 55.488 ms and 60.827 versus 55.077 ms, with unchanged reported area and delay. A bounded cut-rewriting branch finds additional area savings on released and generated functions but fails our predeclared runtime criterion. We distinguish universal equivalence arguments from implementation-specific and empirical claims, and do not infer hidden-case or global-optimality guarantees.
 \end{abstract}
 \begin{IEEEkeywords}
