 \section{Discussion, Threats to Validity, and Conclusions}
+\textbf{What changed the research direction?} Three observations governed the sequence. First, Round 1's 13 missed delay targets showed that exactness does not imply a useful gate architecture; this justified representation diversification. Second, the large area reduction around Round 3's required-time development path showed the importance of optimizing a shared graph under a global deadline, while the sequential nature of that record precludes isolated causal attribution. Third, the small public-case area gains and large synthesis-time penalties of the cut branch motivated a predeclared stopping rule, not another unbounded parameter sweep.
+
+\textbf{Why freeze Round 5.1?} The lowest archived area and the selected engineering baseline are not identical. R4.1 Strict recorded $528{,}910$, just $104$ below the R5.1 total $529{,}014$ (about $0.020\%$). R5.1 was selected for engineering efficiency, supported by within-run paired comparisons with R5, while its comparison to R4.1 Strict is not a same-run runtime experiment. R6.1 yielded another modest area reduction but breached the \emph{predeclared} local median-runtime tolerance. Without a published numerical grading formula, this is a transparent design choice, not a claim that R5.1 maximizes the official score.
+
 We distinguish four levels of evidence. Boolean identities and structural inductions prove function preservation for correctly applied transformations. Gate parsing, topological invariants and full-DAG rescoring address implementation correctness. The official evaluator and independent simulator provide empirical checks of emitted circuits. Finally, comparisons over released and synthetic instances support \emph{measured} performance findings. No level proves global minimum-area synthesis for arbitrary functions, optimal search under deadline constraints, or success on unseen case distributions.
